.TH CLOG_CREATE_FROM_FD_MP 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_create_from_fd_mp, clog_create_from_fd \- create a logger writing to an existing file descriptor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_create_from_fd_mp(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                  ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline clog clog_create_from_fd(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to a file descriptor that is already
open.  The logger does not own the file descriptor, so clog_close()
does NOT close it.  A logger on an fd offers no log rotation.

Use fd=1 for stdout and fd=2 for stderr.  Any other open writable fd
works as well, a pipe and a connected socket included; clog_set_format(3)
covers the CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG case.  If the peer end of fd goes away later,
a write after that fails and its record is lost, but by default that
does not stop the process either, because the logger writes to a socket
with MSG_NOSIGNAL and keeps the SIGPIPE of a pipe away from the process.
For a pipe, the first write from a thread other than the async writer
thread sets SIGPIPE to SIG_IGN for the process while its disposition is
still SIG_DFL.  Opening the logger on a pipe changes nothing, and a
logger on a regular file never touches SIGPIPE.  When fd names a regular
file, the open sets SIGXFSZ to SIG_IGN for the process instead, while
its disposition is still SIG_DFL, so a write at the file size limit of
the process (RLIMIT_FSIZE) fails with EFBIG instead of ending the
process.  clog_set_signal_policy(3) has the details, and the policy that
leaves both signals entirely to the application.

fd may be in blocking or in non-blocking mode.  When a write on a
non-blocking fd reports EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK, the calling thread waits
until fd is writable again and tries again, so the rest of the record is
not dropped.  A record is dropped only on a write error that the library
really cannot recover from.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
fd         Open, writable file descriptor. Must be >= 0.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped. CLOG_OFF
           suppresses all output except CLOG_FATAL, which is always
           written.
async_cfg  The configuration for async logging, or NULL for
           synchronous logging.  When it is not NULL, a clog_<level>()
           call formats only its own message and hands the rest to a
           dedicated writer thread, and every logger that clog_derive()
           makes from this logger logs asynchronously too.  See
           clog_async_cfg_t below.  Unlike a rotation config, a struct
           of all zeros also turns async mode on, with a default for
           each zero field; only a literal NULL keeps the logging
           synchronous.
             clog_async_cfg_t fields:
               queue_size        - the capacity of the message
                                    queue.  0 means an unbounded
                                    queue that never blocks a caller
                                    on a send, and a value above 0
                                    means a bounded queue of that
                                    many messages.  A full bounded
                                    queue BLOCKS the caller until
                                    there is space again; it never
                                    drops a message.
               flush_buffer_size - a count of bytes.  The writer
                                    thread flushes once its
                                    aggregation buffer reaches this
                                    size.  0 gives
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_BUFFER_SIZE.
                                    Each record has an internal cap
                                    of 16 MiB, but a value above that
                                    cap still applies, and the growth
                                    ceiling of the aggregation buffer
                                    follows it.
               flush_interval_us - a uint64_t count of
                                    microseconds.  The writer thread
                                    flushes at least this often, even
                                    when the buffer is below
                                    flush_buffer_size and while
                                    records keep the queue busy
                                    without a pause.  0 gives
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_INTERVAL_US
                                    (200 ms).  Every value of the
                                    type is valid.  UINT64_MAX, or
                                    any interval too long for the
                                    clock to reach, turns the time
                                    trigger off, and then only
                                    flush_buffer_size, clog_flush()
                                    and clog_close() start a flush.
                                    Whatever the interval, a writer
                                    thread with nothing to deliver
                                    (an empty buffer and no repair
                                    owed to the output) sleeps until
                                    a record or a request arrives.
mprocs     A custom allocator with function pointers for malloc, free,
           calloc and realloc, or NULL for the default system
           allocator.  When mprocs is not NULL all four pointers must
           be set; a struct that is only partly filled makes the call
           return CLOG_NULL and no logger is created.  The library
           copies the struct, so you can free yours after the call.
           Every logger that clog_derive() makes from this logger uses
           the same allocator.  The two allocations that belong to the
           library, the symbol text of a backtrace and the identity
           cache of each thread, use malloc(3); see clogger(7).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new logger handle on success.
CLOG_NULL when fd is negative, closed, read-only or not valid in
some other way, when min_level is not one of the values of
clog_level_t, when mprocs is not valid, or when a memory allocation
fails.  CLOG_NULL also comes back when the process is ending and the
library has already released its table of loggers, which is what an
open from a destructor that runs after the destructor of the library
gets.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library does not duplicate the fd, so you must keep it open for the
whole life of the logger.
Every public function is fully thread-safe.  One lock, shared by the
root logger and every logger derived from it, serialises every write to
a given target.
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with clogger:
  - call fork(2) before you open a logger;
  - open a logger, call fork(2), and then call exec(3) immediately.
A process that goes on without exec(3) while a handle from before the
fork is live is not using a supported pattern.

As a defence in depth, a handle that is open at the time of such a fork
can still be used in the child, and log, derive and close work
correctly on both sides.  The async writer thread of a logger does not
exist in a forked child, so an inherited handle with async mode on
switches to synchronous logging in the child, without the caller
noticing.

This fallback is part of the fork protection of the library, which a
build with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 leaves out, and such a process
must not fork while it holds a live async logger.

When async_cfg is not NULL, CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG output is not batched,
whatever queue_size, flush_buffer_size and flush_interval_us say, and
every record and every backtrace frame keeps its own write() call.
Every format is exempt from batching on a message-oriented socket (a
SOCK_DGRAM, SOCK_SEQPACKET or SOCK_RDM socket, such as a UDP socket or
a Unix datagram socket), which the logger detects when it opens.  Such
a socket delivers each write as one message, so every record goes out
as a message of its own, exactly as a synchronous logger sends it.  A
record larger than the socket can carry fails alone: a "log record
truncated: 0 of <m> bytes written" record takes its place, and the
records after it are still delivered.  When the receiver has no room
the write waits for room, both where the system blocks the send and
where it refuses the send with ENOBUFS, as FreeBSD does for a Unix
datagram socket; the logger sends again until the message fits, and a
CLOG_FATAL call or the drain at exit waits only within its time budget.
A CLOG_FATAL call always goes past the queue.  It drains everything
that earlier calls already queued, writes the fatal record itself
synchronously, and only then stops the process.

When a process ends with exit(3), or a return from main(), while an
async logger is still open, an exit handler that the library registers
when it loads drains every async logger of the process the way a
CLOG_FATAL call does: the queue first, then the batch, with a "log
record truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>):
the process stopped" record for what cannot be delivered.  The handler
runs after every exit handler that the application registers later, so
a record that such a handler logs is delivered too.  A budget of 5
seconds bounds the whole drain, so a descriptor that takes nothing
more, or threads that keep logging, delay the exit by that much at
most.  The budget also bounds every lock that the drain takes.  A
logger whose lock another thread holds past the budget is skipped, and
the records it still holds can be lost.  A thread that logs while the
drain runs writes its record directly, after the records it queued
earlier; it waits for the lock of the logger only until the drain's
own deadline, or 100 ms when that is later, and drops the record if
the lock doesn't come in that time.  Every write of the drain is
bounded as well.  A socket is written with MSG_DONTWAIT, and on macOS,
whose send(2) ignores that flag, a stream socket in blocking mode is
polled for room before each write of at most SO_SNDLOWAT bytes.  A pipe
or a FIFO is polled for room before each write of at most PIPE_BUF
bytes.  A terminal is written through a second, non-blocking open of
the same device, because room on a terminal does not mean room for a
whole write, and the descriptor of the logger keeps its own mode.  So a
descriptor in blocking mode whose reader stopped reading holds up the
exit no longer than the budget allows.  The drain takes the lock of a
logger only when that logger has a queue or a compression to drain, so
a thread that blocks for ever inside a write of a synchronous logger
delays neither an exit nor a CLOG_FATAL call.
If the writer thread of a logger reaches exit(3) itself, from inside a
custom allocator, the drain of that one logger is skipped, because that
thread cannot serve it.  A record that an async logger receives after
its drain (from an exit handler that runs after the library's, or from
a destructor) is written synchronously by the thread that logs it, and
clog_close(3) from there still works.  A logger whose drain the budget
cut off, because its writer thread did not answer in time, keeps its
queue, and such a record can then be lost: an unbounded queue still
takes it, a bounded queue drops it at once (its sends stay off, full or
not), clog_flush(3) returns at once, and clog_close(3) does not wait
for the writer thread either but leaves the logger in place for the end
of the process.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no exit handler,
so call clog_flush(3) or clog_close(3) first where the queued records
matter.

The kernel can accept only part of a write, for example when a peer
goes away in the middle of a record or a filesystem has just run out of
space.  The bytes it did take then go out without the terminating
newline of that record.  A synchronous logger writes that newline
itself, so the next record starts on a line of its own and is never
joined onto the fragment, and then it writes a "log record truncated:
<n> of <m> bytes written" record that names the loss.  An async logger
keeps the bytes that the write did not take in its batching buffer and
offers them again on the next flush.  They are the exact continuation
of what did get out, so once the condition clears the logger completes
the record and delivers every record behind it.

The newline and the marker of a loss go to the output that has just
failed, and on a full disk they usually fail too.  The logger then
keeps both and writes them in front of the next write that the output
accepts.  An async logger also tries them at each flush of its writer
thread, so they don't wait for a new record, and every logger tries
them at exit(3) when nothing is written again: the newline first, then
one marker.  While a single loss waits, the marker carries its own
text; for more than one it reads "log record truncated: <k> earlier
losses (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>", with the number
of losses, the records and the bytes they lost, and the errno of the
last write that failed.  A record that arrives while that newline is
still waiting is not written, because it would continue the fragment on
the same line, and it counts as one more loss.  So every line of the
log holds at most one record, and every loss is named once the output
has room again.  An error that no retry gets past, such as EPIPE from a
pipe whose reader is gone, ends the wait: the output is gone, so the
logger gives up the newline and the marker.  The last clog_close(3) of
the logger makes one more attempt within 100 milliseconds, and a loss
that it cannot name then stays unnamed.

A flush keeps the undelivered bytes only after an error that a later
attempt can get past: ENOSPC, EDQUOT, EFBIG, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM, or EAGAIN
or EINTR that ends a wait.  Any other write error, such as EPIPE,
ECONNRESET or EBADF, fails the same way on every retry, so the flush
drops the undelivered bytes and writes a "log record truncated:
undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>"
record in their place, and the records after it go out as usual.  The
last flush of clog_close(3) has no later retry, so what it cannot
deliver is named the same way, with "the logger closed" as the reason.
A record that does not fit into the batching buffer behind the bytes
that a flush keeps is a loss as well, and a "log record truncated: a
record did not fit behind the undelivered batch (<n> byte message)"
record names it; the library never calls such a record too large,
because the record itself is small.

Some records have to reach the descriptor before those bytes do, and
the library never writes such a record between the two halves of the
record that the bytes belong to.  There are two such cases, a
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG record, which is never batched, and a record that takes
the synchronous write path.  Each of them first gives the outstanding
bytes one more delivery attempt of its own.  If the descriptor still
won't take them, it gives up on them and on every remaining line of the
split record, backtrace lines included, and writes the missing newline
of the split record (when a line is open) and a "log record truncated:
<n> undelivered bytes dropped" record in their place.  A logfmt record
whose backtrace lines are still outstanding counts as split even when
the bytes already written end with the newline of its first line.  The
records that the batch holds behind the split one are kept, and a later
flush delivers them, so they follow the record that had to be written
past them.
.fi
